# OAuth2 token introspection

```sh
ddd recipe oauth2-token-introspection --path <service> --dry-run
ddd recipe oauth2-token-introspection --path <service>
```

The recipe adds a dependency-free RFC7662 client, sync/async Nest module, barrel,
consumer tests and this document. It does not wire AppModule, install packages,
change lockfiles or grant access. Dry-run writes nothing. All targets are checked
before writes; differing files and symlinks fail closed, identical reruns are
untouched, and failed writes roll back unchanged files created by this invocation.
Rollback checks inode, timestamps and content; concurrent owner changes are
preserved and reported as an explicit rollback conflict.

## Configuration and composition

Import the owning module; do not redeclare its exported client in a consumer.

```ts
OAuth2TokenIntrospectionModule.registerAsync({
  imports: [ConfigModule],
  inject: [ConfigService],
  useFactory: (config: ConfigService): OAuth2TokenIntrospectionOptions => ({
    introspectionUrl: config.getOrThrow<string>('OAUTH2_INTROSPECTION_URL'),
    requestHeaders: { authorization: config.getOrThrow<string>('OAUTH2_INTROSPECTION_AUTHORIZATION') },
    timeoutMs: 3000,
  }),
})
```

Set the exact trusted URL and required provider authentication headers from
validated deployment configuration. Never derive the URL or headers from a
request. HTTPS is the default. Credentials, queries and fragments in the URL
are rejected; redirects are disabled. For a verified private HTTP endpoint,
explicitly set `trustedInternalHttp: true`. For example, self-hosted Hydra v26.2.0
can use `http://hydra-admin.ory-staging.svc.cluster.local:4445/admin/oauth2/introspect`.
This opt-in does not add TLS or secure a cluster: the operator must establish
private routing and network policy and keep admin ports off public ingress.
Hydra's admin listener uses private network protection; do not assume a Basic
header authenticates it. Omit `requestHeaders` when the selected server does not
require them. No introspection endpoint is discovered from token contents.

Options snapshot URL/headers and bound timeout (10..30000 ms, default 3000),
response bytes (128..1048576, default 65536) and token bytes (1..65536, default
16384). Tokens must be nonempty visible ASCII without a Bearer prefix. A timeout
includes transport and response streaming; even a noncompliant transport cannot
hold the caller indefinitely. `transport(url, RequestInit)` is an injectable
Fetch-compatible seam for tests or an owned transport; it must honor the URL,
AbortSignal and redirect policy. No retries or positive-validation cache exist.
Every call rechecks the provider. Runtime baseline is Node 18+ or Bun with Fetch
and Web Streams; no consumer dependency installation is needed.

## Bounded provider JSON GET

The barrel also exports `BoundedJsonHttpClient`, its options and
`BoundedJsonHttpUnavailableError`. Introspection delegates to this same mechanism;
provider adapters do not need to copy timeout, stream limits or HTTP error logic.
Each instance has an exact trusted origin from validated deployment config, with
no path, query, fragment or credentials. Its GET API accepts path segments and a
bounded query record; it percent-encodes segments and rejects dot traversal.
No call can select another origin. No bearer token or body is sent on GET.

```ts
const kratos = new BoundedJsonHttpClient({
  origin: config.getOrThrow<string>('KRATOS_ADMIN_ORIGIN'),
  trustedInternalHttp: true, // only for the verified private cluster endpoint
  timeoutMs: 3000,
});
// The owning adapter validates sessionId/identityId as UUIDs before these calls.
const sessionJson = await kratos.get(['admin', 'sessions', sessionId], { expand: 'identity' });
const identityJson = await kratos.get(['admin', 'identities', identityId]);
```

Use only configured origin and fixed resource route segments, followed by encoded
identifiers validated by the consumer. Never pass an origin from request data.
The return type is unknown JSON; the consumer validates provider schema, active
session/identity, expiry, assurance, binding and authorization. The mechanism
does not infer account status from JSON. Transport errors retain safe reason and
optional numeric HTTP status, so the adapter can distinguish a missing session
from upstream unavailability without inspecting or logging response bodies.
The helper also exposes bounded `postForm` for the introspection client; it
applies the same origin, redirect, timeout, byte, nesting and no-cache rules.

## Evidence and consumer policy

`introspect(originalAccessToken)` POSTs form-encoded `token` and
`token_type_hint=access_token`. The hint does not prove token kind. Active false
throws `InvalidOAuth2TokenError`; active true returns immutable evidence with
optional issuer, subject, client ID, numeric dates, token type/use, audience and
scope arrays, raw claims, and provider extensions. RFC7662 permits missing
claims; the client does not invent them or map them into people or permissions.

Prefer `validate(token, policy)` to return only a consumer-approved principal:

```ts
const principal = await client.validate(token, {
  validate: async (evidence) => {
    // The owner must enforce the selected provider's required issuer, resource
    // audience, authorized client, time, access-token profile and actor contract.
    // Then check canonical identity, current session/revocation and domain scope.
    if (evidence.issuer !== trustedIssuer || evidence.tokenUse !== 'access_token') {
      throw new InvalidOAuth2TokenError('policy_rejected');
    }
    return owningAdmissionPolicy.resolve(evidence);
  },
});
```

The example delegates the remaining checks; matching issuer and token_use alone
is insufficient. No generic grant-type or human/machine inference is made.
ZITADEL JWT validation remains a separate supported recipe; this transport must
not become an automatic fallback after a rejected token. For Hydra v26.2.0,
introspection returns `client_id`, `token_use`, `aud` and `ext`; arbitrary
consent `session.access_token` data is returned in `ext` and carried through
refresh grants. A Kratos binding in that data is a consumer-defined convention,
not a built-in introspection session field. Hydra and Kratos session revocation
are separate; the owner must validate any bound Kratos session independently.

## Failures and proof

`InvalidOAuth2TokenError` means malformed, inactive or explicitly policy-rejected
credentials; map to the consumer's denial response. `OAuth2IntrospectionUnavailableError`
means timeout, transport, HTTP, malformed upstream response or unexpected policy
failure; deny access while distinguishing an unavailable validator operationally.
Errors contain fixed messages and safe reason enums, never tokens, headers,
claims, URLs or upstream bodies. The client performs no logging. Explicit policy
errors preserve these safe classes; unexpected exceptions are sanitized.
Policy implementations own their own I/O limits and must not log credentials.

The generated spec verifies wire format, immutable evidence, no validation
cache, denial, HTTP opt-in, malformed/bounded responses, hangs, error redaction,
consumer policy and module ownership. It proves a transport contract, not
deployment, provider authentication, clinical permissions or real actor access.

Sources: [RFC7662](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7662),
[pinned Hydra API](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ory/hydra/v26.2.0/spec/api.json),
[pinned Hydra admin server](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ory/hydra/v26.2.0/cmd/server/handler.go).
